+#![allow(dead_code)]
+// the stub keeps the values the implementation will read; the reference
+// solution drops the allowance, because by then every one is used.
+
+//! The state machine: a pure function from the log to the state.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.10-deterministic-state-machine` is where the log finally becomes a key-value store.
+//! Everything before this step moved entries around; nothing yet said what an entry *means*.
+//!
+//! The rule that makes a replicated store possible is one sentence: **the state is a function of the
+//! entries, and of nothing else.** Two nodes that applied the same entries in the same order hold
+//! the same state, byte for byte — and a snapshot (`5.4`) can carry that state because it is a fact,
+//! not an opinion. Every source of non-determinism breaks that sentence, and the usual suspects are
+//! boring and fatal:
+//!
+//! * **iteration order over a hash map.** Two nodes insert the same keys in the same order and
+//!   iterate them differently; if the state's *serialised* form depends on that order, the two
+//!   replicas disagree about their own digest while holding the same data.
+//! * **the clock.** A state machine that reads `now` for a TTL applies a different function on
+//!   every replay, and a follower that replays the log after a restart disagrees with the leader
+//!   about what happened.
+//! * **randomness** and **floating point** — the first has no place in a replicated function at all,
+//!   the second agrees with itself only on the same platform and the same instruction set.
+//!
+//! So the state is an *ordered* map, the digest walks it in key order, and the API has no time
+//! argument anywhere. That last one is a type-level statement, and it is the one a test cannot
+//! check — which is why the step's page says it in words and the code says it by having nowhere to
+//! put a clock.
+//!
+//! The state machine also refuses to be applied out of order. `apply` takes the entry's index and
+//! refuses anything that is not exactly the next one: a state machine that accepted a gap would be
+//! a state machine whose state depends on which entries happened to arrive, which is the one thing
+//! this step exists to rule out.
+
+use std::collections::BTreeMap;
+
+use crate::error::Result;
+use crate::raft::Index;
+
+/// One write, as the state machine understands it.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Command {
+    /// Set a key.
+    Set {
+        /// The key.
+        key: String,
+        /// The value.
+        value: Vec<u8>,
+    },
+    /// Set a key only if it currently holds `expect`.
+    Cas {
+        /// The key.
+        key: String,
+        /// The value the key must hold.
+        expect: Vec<u8>,
+        /// The value to write.
+        value: Vec<u8>,
+    },
+    /// Remove a key.
+    Delete {
+        /// The key.
+        key: String,
+    },
+}
+
+impl Command {
+    /// Parse a command from an entry's payload: `set k v`, `cas k expect new`, `delete k`, with
+    /// values as hex.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
